# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  🔴 READ THE TOP BLOCK BEFORE COPYING THIS FILE
#
#  The hazards come FIRST, before any ordinary configuration. That ordering is
#  deliberate: on a prior engagement the dangerous variable was documented
#  halfway down a file nobody read to the end of, and a development machine
#  started writing to live production records within seconds of first boot.
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ── 🔴 1. BACKGROUND WORKERS — OFF ON EVERY COPY THAT IS NOT THE HOST ────────
#
# Gates like this frequently DEFAULT TO ON:
#     const WORKERS_ENABLED = process.env.WORKERS_ENABLED !== "false";
#
# So an unset variable means the full worker fleet starts. A second copy running
# against a live database does not fail loudly — it double-sends customer
# messages, double-writes records, and fights the real deployment for shared
# watermarks.
#
# Assume they start. Prove otherwise before believing anything else.
WORKERS_ENABLED=false

# ── 🔴 2. ANYTHING THAT CAN SEND, SPEND OR MESSAGE A REAL CUSTOMER ───────────
#
# Leave these UNSET locally. An SMS key, an email key, a payments key or a
# webhook sender configured on a dev machine will reach real people.
#
# TODO: list them here by name, unset, with one line each on what they can do.
# SMS_API_KEY=            # can send texts to real customers
# EMAIL_API_KEY=          # can send email to real customers
# PAYMENTS_API_KEY=       # can move money

# ── 🔴 3. DATABASE — READ-ONLY ROLE ONLY ─────────────────────────────────────
#
# This must be a SELECT-only role on production. Never the owner credential.
# Never ask the client to "just paste the DATABASE_URL and I'll sort the rest" —
# that puts the production owner credential on your machine and defeats the
# entire point.
#
# Verify it: reads work, and CREATE TABLE / UPDATE / DELETE are all REFUSED.
#
# ⚠️ In a cloud session the Postgres port does not connect — traffic goes
#    through an HTTP proxy and it HANGS rather than failing. Use the provider's
#    HTTPS SQL endpoint. See .claude/rules/GENERAL-querying-live-data.md
#
# ⚠️ The hosting platform's shell often points at a DIFFERENT database with the
#    same schema. Confirm the host before drawing any conclusion:
#        node -e "console.log(new URL(process.env.DATABASE_URL).host)"
DATABASE_URL=postgres://readonly_analyst:PASSWORD@HOST/DBNAME?sslmode=require

# ── 4. AUTH ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Where a dev bypass exists it should fail CLOSED — two conditions, neither
# inferred:
#     AUTH_DEV_BYPASS === "1" && NODE_ENV === "development"
#
# A gate written as NODE_ENV !== "production" fails OPEN: an unset or misspelled
# NODE_ENV turns every request into a full admin.
#
# If this is missing, the UI loads and every API call 401s, and the page spins
# forever. It looks like slow data. It is a locked door.
NODE_ENV=development
AUTH_DEV_BYPASS=1

# ── 5. ORDINARY CONFIGURATION ────────────────────────────────────────────────
PORT=8080

# TODO: everything else. Most variables in these codebases sit behind
# isConfigured()-style guards and degrade gracefully — count how many are
# genuinely REQUIRED and say so. On a prior engagement the code read around
# seventy and hard-required two.
